  "solution": {
    "text": "For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>, the radial part of the wave function is:<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#936;</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></msup></math><br />Probability of finding an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>&#8211;</mo></msup></math> in a spherical shell of thickness, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>'</mo><mi>dr</mi><mo>'</mo></math>&nbsp;at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>'</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>'</mo></math> from the nucleus:<br /><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#936;</mi></mrow><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></math><br /><math><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></math><br />So, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> is zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8734;</mi></math>.<br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/41561564465105.jpg\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 112px;\" /><br />So, the plot of radial probability function, <math><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mo>.</mo><msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#936;</mi></mrow><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></math>&nbsp;V/s&nbsp;<math><mtext>r</mtext></math> is as shown above.",
